\use{document}

\set vsize {300}

\title {Cost-Based Page Breaking}
\author {The texish project}
\date {June 2026}
\maketitle

\section {Choosing a break by cost}

The page builder now chooses where to break by cost, as TeX does, rather than
simply taking the latest legal break that happens to fit. The cost of a break is
the page's badness there --- how hard the page glue must work to reach the page
height --- plus the penalty at the break. A positive penalty makes a discouraged
break less attractive even when it would fit, so a break that would strand
material at the foot of a page gives way to a better one above it.

On an ordinary page, where the interline glue is rigid, every break that fits is
equally good, so the cost rule simply takes the last one that fits --- exactly
the old behaviour, unchanged. The difference shows only where the glue can flex
or a penalty intervenes.

This short page measure makes the breaks easy to see: the body is only three
hundred points tall, so the text spills onto a second page after a few
paragraphs, and each page is filled to a sensible break rather than to the very
last line that would squeeze in.

The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog, and then, gathering its breath, it
turns and jumps back again, because a single leap is never quite enough to settle
an old argument between a fox and a dog who have known each other far too long.

